"R8-2020-0006 Regional De Minimis Permit (https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/santaana/board_decisions/adopted_orders/orders/2020/R8-2020-0006.pdf)\nciteturn1087view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 51; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1077view2\",\"lineno\":10}); Total lines: 1765\nL0@P0: Individuals, agencies, and/or other parties who discharge\nL1@P0: wastewater that pose an insignificant (de minimis) threat to water\nL2@P0: quality of surface waters.\nL3@P0: Dischargers\nL4@P0: CALIFORNIA REGIONAL WATER QUALITY CONTROL BOARD\nL5@P0: SANTA ANA REGION\nL6@P0: 3737 Main Street, Suite 500, Riverside, California 92501-3348\nL7@P0: Phone (951) 782-4130  FAX (951) 781-6288  TDD (951) 782-3221\nL8@P0: www.waterboards.ca.gov/santaana\nL9@P0: ORDER NO. R8-2020-0006\nL10@P0: NPDES NO. CAG998001\nL11@P0: GENERAL WASTE DISCHARGE REQUIREMENTS FOR DISCHARGES TO SURFACE\nL12@P0: WATERS THAT POSE AN INSIGNIFICANT (DE MINIMIS) THREAT TO WATER QUALITY\nL13@P0: A Discharger, as described in the following table, who has complied with the requirements\nL14@P0: for coverage under this Order, is authorized to discharge under this Order, once the\nL15@P0: Executive Officer has issued an authorization letter, as described in this Order.\nL16@P0: This Order was adopted by the Regional Water Quality\nL17@P0: Control Board on: June 19, 2020\nL18@P0: This Order shall become effective on: July 1, 2020\nL19@P0: This Order shall expire on: June 30, 2025\nL20@P0: The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (USEPA) and the Regional Water Quality\nL21@P0: Control Board have classified the discharges regulated by this Order as minor\nL22@P0: discharges.\nL23@P0: IT IS HEREBY ORDERED, that this Order supersedes Order No. R8-2015-0004 except for\nL24@P0: enforcement purposes, and, in order to meet the provisions contained in division 7 of the\nL25@P0: California Water Code (commencing with Section 13000) and regulations adopted thereunder,\nL26@P0: and the provisions of the federal Clean Water Act (CWA) and regulations and guidelines\nL27@P0: adopted thereunder, the Discharger shall comply with the requirements in this Order.\nL28@P0: I, Hope A. Smythe, Executive Officer, do hereby certify that this Order with all attachments is a\nL29@P0: full, true, and correct copy of an Order adopted by the California Regional Water Quality\nL30@P0: Control Board, Santa Ana Region, on June 19, 2020.\nL31@P0: Hope A. Smythe, Executive Officer\nL32@P0-1: Order Page 1 of 12Order No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES No. CAG998001\nL33@P1: General WDRs for Insignificant Threat Discharges to\nL34@P1: Surface Waters Page 2 of 12\nL35@P1: TABLE OF CONTENTS\nL36@P1: I. Discharge Information ....................................................................................................... 3\nL37@P1: II. Application Information ...................................................................................................... 4\nL38@P1: III. Findings............................................................................................................................. 4\nL39@P1: IV. Discharge Prohibitions....................................................................................................... 6\nL40@P1: V. Effluent Limitations and Discharge Specifications ............................................................. 7\nL41@P1: A. Effluent Limitations...................................................................................................... 7\nL42@P1: B. Land Discharge Specifications – Not Applicable......................................................... 7\nL43@P1: C. Reclamation Specifications – Not Applicable.............................................................. 7\nL44@P1: VI. Receiving Water Limitations .............................................................................................. 7\nL45@P1: A. Surface Water Limitations........................................................................................... 7\nL46@P1: B. Groundwater Limitations ............................................................................................. 8\nL47@P1: VII. Provisions.......................................................................................................................... 9\nL48@P1: A. Standard Provisions.................................................................................................... 9\nL49@P1: B. Monitoring and Reporting Program Requirements.................................................... 11\nL50@P1: C. Reopener Provisions................................................................................................. 12\nL51@P1: List of Tables\nL52@P1: Table 1. Effluent Limitations Applicable to All Receiving Waters .......................................... 7\nL53@P1: List of Attachments\nL54@P1: Attachment A – Definitions .....................................................................................................A-1\nL55@P1: Attachment B – Notice of Intent..............................................................................................B-1\nL56@P1: Attachment C – Not Used.......................................................................................................C-1\nL57@P1: Attachment D – Federal Standard Provisions.........................................................................D-1\nL58@P1: Attachment E – Monitoring and Reporting Program (MRP)....................................................E-1\nL59@P1: Attachment F – Fact Sheet..................................................................................................... F-1\nL60@P1-2: Order Page 2 of 12Order No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES No. CAG998001\nL61@P2: General WDRs for Insignificant Threat Discharges to Surface Waters Page 3 of 12\nL62@P2: I. DISCHARGE INFORMATION\nL63@P2: 1. The types of wastewater discharges regulated under this Order include the following:\nL64@P2: a. Construction dewatering wastes;1\nL65@P2: b. Wastes associated with well installation, development, test pumping and purging 2;\nL66@P2: c. Aquifer testing wastes;\nL67@P2: d. Dewatering wastes from subterranean seepage3(except for discharges from utility\nL68@P2: vaults);\nL69@P2: e. Discharges resulting from hydrostatic testing of vessels, pipelines, tanks, etc.2;\nL70@P2: f. Discharges resulting from the maintenance of potable water supply pipelines,tanks,\nL71@P2: reservoirs, etc.;2\nL72@P2: g. Discharges resulting from the disinfection of potable water supply pipelines,tanks,\nL73@P2: reservoirs, etc.2;\nL74@P2: h. Discharges from potable water supply systems resulting from initial system startup,\nL75@P2: routine startup, sampling of influent flow, system failures, pressure releases,etc.2;\nL76@P2: i. Discharges from fire hydrant testing or flushing2;\nL77@P2: j. Air conditioning condensate;\nL78@P2: k. Swimming pool discharge;\nL79@P2: l. Discharges resulting from diverted stream flows;\nL80@P2: m. Decanted filter backwash wastewater and/or sludge dewatering filtrate waterfrom\nL81@P2: water treatment facilities; and\nL82@P2: n. Other similar types of wastes as determined by the Regional Water BoardExecutive\nL83@P2: Officer, which pose a de minimis threat to water quality yet must be regulated under\nL84@P2: waste discharge requirements.\nL85@P2: 2. This Order regulates proposed groundwater related discharges and/or de minimis\nL86@P2: discharges within the San Diego Creek/Newport Bay Watershed that do not contain\nL87@P2: nutrients, selenium, and other pollutants of TMDL concern at levels that pose a threat to\nL88@P2: water quality.\nL89@P2: 3. The following discharges are excluded from regulation under this Order:\nL90@P2: a. Wastewater with pollutants of concern other than those for which effluentlimitations\nL91@P2: are specified in this Order.\nL92@P2: 1 Except for storm water discharges regulated under a statewide general constructionstorm\nL93@P2: water permit or a Municipal Separate Storm Sewer System permit.\nL94@P2: 2 Except for wastes discharged from water purveyors regulated under statewidegeneral\nL95@P2: waste discharge requirements, such as Order WQ 2014-0194-DWQ, NPDES No.\nL96@P2: CAG140001.\nL97@P2: 3 Except for waste discharges from utility vaults regulated under statewide general waste\nL98@P2: discharge requirements, such as Order No. 2014-0174-DWQ, NPDES No.CAG990002.\nL99@P2-3: De Minimis OrderOrder No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES No. CAG998001\nL100@P3: General WDRs for Insignificant Threat Discharges to Surface Waters Page 4 of 12\nL101@P3: b. Wastewater discharges from hydro-testing of contaminated pipes, vessels or tanks.\nL102@P3: c. Wastewater discharges from draining of decorative ponds, golf course lakes, etc.,\nL103@P3: unless full characterization of the wastewater for the presence of pesticides,\nL104@P3: insecticides, biocides and/or other chemicals that may have been applied to the\nL105@P3: water is provided which shows that there are no pollutants present at levels of\nL106@P3: concern.\nL107@P3: II. APPLICATION INFORMATION\nL108@P3: A. Existing Dischargers\nL109@P3: Existing Dischargers previously authorized to discharge under Order No. R8-2015-0004\nL110@P3: who wish to continue their discharge(s) and be regulated under the terms and\nL111@P3: conditions of this Order must complete sections I., II., III., IV., and VIII. of Attachment B\nL112@P3: and submit it, no later than August 1, 2020. If no application is submitted by that date,\nL113@P3: coverage to discharge shall be terminated and a complete Notice of Intent, with a new\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNewport Bay Draft Time Schedule Order R8-2025-0003, Amendment To Time Schedule Order R8-2019-0050, As Amended by Order R8-2023-0063 (https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/santaana/board_decisions/adopted_orders/orders/2025/r8-2025-0003.pdf)\nciteturn1087view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1082view0\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 541\nL0@P0: CALIFORNIA REGIONAL WATER QUALITY CONTROL\nL1@P0: BOARD SANTA ANA REGION\nL2@P0: ORDER R8-2025-0003\nL3@P0: AMENDMENT TO TIME SCHEDULE ORDER R8-2019-0050, AS\nL4@P0: PREVIOUSLY AMENDED BY ORDER R8-2023-0063\nL5@P0: TO COMPLY WITH\nL6@P0: THE REQUIREMENTS PRESCRIBED IN ORDER R8-2009-0030, AS\nL7@P0: AMENDED BY ORDER R8-2010-0062\nL8@P0: (NPDES PERMIT NUMBER CAS618030)\nL9@P0: FINDINGS\nL10@P0: The Santa Ana Regional Water Quality Control Board (Santa Ana Water\nL11@P0: Board) hereby finds as follows:\nL12@P0: 1. On May 22, 2009, the Santa Ana Water Board adopted Waste\nL13@P0: Discharge Requirements (Order R8-2009-0030) and National Pollutant\nL14@P0: Discharge Elimination System (NPDES) Permit No. CAS618030 for\nL15@P0: owners or operators of municipal separate storm sewer systems (MS4s)\nL16@P0: within the County of Orange and incorporated cities therein (Orange\nL17@P0: County MS4 Permit).\nL18@P0: 2. On October 29, 2010, the Orange County MS4 Permit was amended\nL19@P0: by Order R8-2010-0062. The Orange County MS4 Permit expired on\nL20@P0: May 22, 2014, but the permit was administratively extended and\nL21@P0: continues to be in effect until the Santa Ana Water Board adopts a new\nL22@P0: permit. The waste load allocations (WLAs) for Total Maximum Daily\nL23@P0: Loads for Fecal Coliform in Newport Bay (Fecal Coliform TMDL) were\nL24@P0: incorporated into the Orange County MS4 Permit as water quality\u0002based effluent limitations.\nL25@P0: 3. The Santa Ana Water Board adopted a Time Schedule Order (TSO)\nL26@P0: R8-2019-0050 on December 6, 2019. Finding 18 of TSO Order R8-\nL27@P0: 2019-0050 states, in part, “Monitoring data submitted by the County\nL28@P0: of Orange indicates that the Permittees discharging to Newport Bay\nL29@P0-1: are not meeting the fecal coliform [effluent limitations], and the final Time Schedule Order R8-2025-0003 Page 2 of 12\nL30@P1: Amendment to Time Schedule Order R8-2019-0050\nL31@P1: compliance date has passed. Accordingly, pursuant to California\nL32@P1: Water Code section 13300, a discharge of waste is taking place or\nL33@P1: threatens to take place that violates requirements prescribed by the\nL34@P1: Santa Ana Water Board.”\nL35@P1: 4. TSO R8-2019-0050 required that the Permittees must achieve full\nL36@P1: compliance with the Orange County MS4 Permit’s fecal coliform\nL37@P1: water quality-based effluent limitations as soon as possible but no\nL38@P1: later than four years from the adoption of TSO R8-2019-0050. The\nL39@P1: Santa Ana Water Board also authorized the Executive Officer to grant\nL40@P1: an extension of the TSO up to one year by Resolution R8-2019-0050\nL41@P1: and applicable limits prescribed under the California Water Code\nL42@P1: (CWC) section 13385, subdivision (j)(3)(C)(i).\nL43@P1: 5. On December 22, 2023, the Executive Officer adopted Order R8-\nL44@P1: 2023-0063 (Amended TSO). The Amended TSO extended TSO R8-\nL45@P1: 2019-0050 to December 6, 2024, to provide additional time for the\nL46@P1: Permittees to comply with the fecal coliform effluent limitations in the\nL47@P1: 2010 Orange County MS4 Permit. As reported in the 2023 Newport\nL48@P1: Bay Fecal Coliform Annual Data and Progress Report, when\nL49@P1: averaged over multiple stations for upper and lower Newport Bay,\nL50@P1: there are general trends of lower Fecal Coliform levels when\nL51@P1: compared to previous years. However, the WLAs for REC1 are still\nL52@P1: not being met for some monitoring stations specified in the Amended\nL53@P1: TSO.\nL54@P1: 6. On August 6, 2024, the Permittees in the Newport Bay watershed\nL55@P1: submitted a written request to extend TSO R8-2019-0050 beyond\nL56@P1: what was authorized by the Amended TSO. This Order responds to\nL57@P1: that request and, in part, allows another extension but for a period of\nL58@P1: three years.\nL59@P1: 7. The Permittees have developed adaptive approaches to prioritize,\nL60@P1: identify, and control sources of fecal coliform and a method to\nL61@P1: demonstrate the effectiveness of controls by monitoring and reporting.\nL62@P1: The Permittees will implement these through an approved Pollution\nL63@P1: Prevention Plan (PPP). The PPP was conditionally approved by the\nL64@P1: Executive Officer on September 10, 2024. Implementation of the PPP\nL65@P1: is expected to result in compliance with the fecal coliform water\nL66@P1: quality-based effluent limitations within three years of the date of this\nL67@P1: Order.\nL68@P1: 8. Implementation of the PPP will take more time than the Amended\nL69@P1: TSO would allow. Among several specified tasks, the PPP, for\nL70@P1: example, requires the Permittees to evaluate a structural treatment\nL71@P1: control system in the Big Canyon Habitat Restoration and Water\nL72@P1-2: Quality Improvement Project. This system is currently in its Time Schedule Order R8-2025-0003 Page 3 of 12\nL73@P2: Amendment to Time Schedule Order R8-2019-0050\nL74@P2: construction phase. Therefore, the task in the PPP to evaluate this\nL75@P2: system cannot be completed within the specified term of TSO R8-\nL76@P2: 2019-0050, and previously extended TSO R8-2023-0063. This\nL77@P2: particular task and other activities are projected to occur for at least\nL78@P2: three years from the date of approval of the PPP.\nL79@P2: 9. This Order adds tasks 9 and 10 to the list of required tasks in\nL80@P2: Attachment C to the Amended TSO R8-2023-0063. The status of the\nL81@P2: Amended TSO tasks and the additional tasks are outlined in an\nL82@P2: updated Attachment C to this Order.\nL83@P2: a. Task 9 in this Order requires continuation of actions to control\nL84@P2: sources of fecal coliform. The Permittees identified these\nL85@P2: actions resulting from the completion of Task 5b in the\nL86@P2: Amended TSO R8-2023-0063. Task 5b requires targeted\nL87@P2: source identification at specific locations. A report related to\nL88@P2: Task 5b has been submitted. This report identifies various\nL89@P2: sources and proposes follow-up actions, some requiring\nL90@P2: coordination with multiple agencies and jurisdictions.\nL91@P2: b. This Order also requires the Permittees to continue\nL92@P2: implementation of the PPP in accordance with the specified\nL93@P2: conditions of approval and provision 17 of the Amended\nL94@P2: TSO R8-2023-0063. A final revised PPP was submitted on\nL95@P2: July 26, 2024, and conditional approval was provided on\nL96@P2: September 10, 2024. Implementation of the PPP will\nL97@P2: continue and is expected to last through the term of the\nL98@P2: TSO as amended by this Order.\nL99@P2: c. Task 10 in this Order specifies the reporting requirements\nL100@P2: for work that will be completed during the remaining term of\nL101@P2: the TSO. In the Task 5b report, the Permittees proposed\nL102@P2: follow-up actions to control sources and monitoring to\nL103@P2: determine the effectiveness of source control measures. In\nL104@P2: the PPP, the Permittees proposed reporting progress on\nL105@P2: monitoring and identification of sources as a part of the\nL106@P2: Annual Fecal Coliform TMDL Monitoring and Progress\nL107@P2: Reports required under a January 2000 Section 13267\nL108@P2: Order. Annual TMDL reports are submitted on September\nL109@P2: 1st of each year since 2001. The Executive Officer’s\nL110@P2: conditional approval of the PPP requires a six-month\nL111@P2: summary report to be submitted by March 31 of each year\nL112@P2: and a technical report to be included in the annual report\nL113@P2: that adequately demonstrates the effectiveness of source\nL114@P2: identification and controls. Task 10 in this TSO requires that\nL115@P2: reports must be adequate for demonstrating the\nL116@P2-3: effectiveness of source identification and controls to comply Time Schedule Order R8-2025-0003 Page 4 of 12\nL117@P3: Amendment to Time Schedule Order R8-2019-0050\nL118@P3: with the fecal coliform water quality-based effluent\nL119@P3: limitations.\nL120@P3: 10.The Permittees are making diligent progress towards bringing the\nL121@P3: waste discharge into compliance with the fecal coliform water quality\u0002based effluent limitations and have demonstrated that an additional\nL122@P3: three-year period is required to fully comply.\nL123@P3: 11.This Order is being issued to extend the time to comply with the fecal\nL124@P3: coliform water quality-based effluent limitations. The 2010 Orange\nL125@P3: County MS4 Permit is an NPDES permit. Pursuant to Water Code\nL126@P3: section 13389, and sections 15301 and 15321, subdivision (a)(2) of\nL127@P3: Title 14 of the California Code of Regulations issuance of this Order is\nL128@P3: exempt from the provisions of the California Environmental Quality Act\nL129@P3: (Public Resources Code section 21100 et seq.).\nL130@P3: 12.If the Permittees comply with the requirements of the Amended TSO,\nL131@P3: as further amended by this Order, the Santa Ana Water Board does\nL132@P3: not intend to take any enforcement action for violations of the fecal\nL133@P3: coliform water quality-based effluent limitations for Newport Bay in the\nL134@P3: 2010 Orange County MS4 Permit or subsequent permits.\nL135@P3: 13.The Santa Ana Water Board may reopen the TSO at its discretion or at\nL136@P3: the request of any of the identified Permittees if warranted. Lack of\nL137@P3: progress towards compliance with the applicable effluent limitations\nL138@P3: addressed by the TSO shall be a reasonable cause for the Santa Ana\nL139@P3: Water Board to modify the terms and conditions of the TSO or consider\nL140@P3: enforcement alternatives.\nL141@P3: 14.The Amended TSO, as further amended by this Order, including\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTentative Resolution R8-2026-0014 (https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/santaana/board_decisions/adopted_orders/orders/2026/r8-2026-0014.pdf)\nciteturn1087view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1082view1\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 267\nL0@P0: California Regional Water Quality Control Board\nL1@P0: Santa Ana Region\nL2@P0: RESOLUTION NO. R8-2026-0014\nL3@P0: Resolution Amending the Water Quality Control Plan for the Santa Ana River\nL4@P0: Basin to Extend the Schedule for Attaining the Middle Santa Ana River Watershed\nL5@P0: Total Maximum Daily Loads for Bacterial Indicators in Wet Winter Conditions\nL6@P0: WHEREAS, the California Regional Water Quality Control Board, Santa Ana\nL7@P0: Region (hereinafter, Santa Ana Water Board), finds that:\nL8@P0: 1. The federal Clean Water Act (CWA) and the state Porter-Cologne Water Quality\nL9@P0: Control Act (Porter-Cologne Act) requires the Santa Ana Water Board to establish\nL10@P0: water quality standards for each waterbody within its region. (33 U.S.C§ 1313;\nL11@P0: Wat. Code, § 13240.) Water quality standards include beneficial uses, water\nL12@P0: quality objectives (numeric and narrative) to protect those beneficial uses, and the\nL13@P0: antidegradation policy. The Porter-Cologne Act also requires the Santa Ana Water\nL14@P0: Board to adopt programs of implementation to achieve the prescribed water quality\nL15@P0: objectives. (Wat. Code, § 13242.)\nL16@P0: 2. On March 11, 1994, an updated Water Quality Control Plan for the Santa Ana\nL17@P0: River Basin (Basin Plan) was adopted by the Santa Ana Water Board, and was\nL18@P0: approved by the State Water Resources Control Board (State Water Board) on\nL19@P0: July 21, 1994, and approved by the Office of Administrative Law (OAL) on\nL20@P0: January 24, 1995. Subsequent amendments to the Basin Plan have been\nL21@P0: similarly adopted and approved.\nL22@P0: 3. The Basin Plan specifies the following beneficial uses (existing or potential) for the\nL23@P0: Middle Santa Ana River Watershed: body contact recreation (REC-1) and non\u0002body contact recreation (REC-2).\nL24@P0: 4. There are six waterbodies within the Middle Santa Ana River Watershed that are\nL25@P0: on the state section 303(d) list (303(d) list). The five waterbodies include:\nL26@P0: · Santa Ana River Reach 3 – Listed in 1988;\nL27@P0: · Chino Creek, Reach 1 – Listed in 1994;\nL28@P0: · Chino Creek, Reach 2 – Listed in 1998;\nL29@P0: · Mill Creek, Prado Area – Listed in 1994;\nL30@P0: · Cucamonga Creek, Reach 1 – Listed in 1998; and\nL31@P0: · Prado Park Lake – Listed in 1998\nL32@P0-1: 5. The beneficial use adversely affected by elevated fecal coliform densities in 2\nL33@P1: Resolution No. R8-2026-0014\nL34@P1: Page 2 of 7\nL35@P1: Middle Santa Ana River Watershed Waterbodies is REC-1. The Basin Plan\nL36@P1: prescribes the following numeric water quality objectives for the Middle Santa\nL37@P1: Ana River Watershed: Fecal coliform: Log mean less than 200 organisms/100\nL38@P1: mL based on five or more samples per 30-day period, and not more than 10% of\nL39@P1: the samples exceed 400 organisms/100 mL for any 30-day period; E. coli: Log\nL40@P1: mean less than 126 organisms/100 mL based on five or more samples per 30-\nL41@P1: day period, and not more than 10% of the samples exceed 235 organisms/100\nL42@P1: mL for any 30-day period.\nL43@P1: 6. Section 303(d) of the Clean Water Act requires each state to identify the waters\nL44@P1: within its boundaries that do not meet water quality standards. Those waters are\nL45@P1: placed on the state’s section 303(d) list (303(d) list). The Santa Ana Water Board\nL46@P1: is required and authorized to establish a total maximum daily load (TMDL) for\nL47@P1: those pollutants identified as causing impairment of waters within its jurisdiction\nL48@P1: on the 303(d) list. Additionally, under Water Code section 13242, the Santa Ana\nL49@P1: Water Board is required to develop an implementation program to achieve water\nL50@P1: quality objectives.\nL51@P1: 7. The numeric fecal coliform water quality objectives were not being met in Middle\nL52@P1: Santa Ana River Watershed Waterbodies. As a result of violations of the fecal\nL53@P1: coliform objective and beneficial use impacts to the Middle Santa Ana River\nL54@P1: Watershed Waterbodies, on August 26, 2005, the Basin Plan was amended by\nL55@P1: Resolution R8-2005-0001, “Resolution Amending the Water Quality Control Plan\nL56@P1: for the Santa Ana River Basin to Incorporate Bacterial Indicator Total Maximum\nL57@P1: Daily Loads (TMDLs) for Middle Santa Ana River Watershed Waterbodies.” The\nL58@P1: Basin Plan amendment received final approval from U.S. EPA on May 16, 2007.\nL59@P1: The amendment was added to Chapter 6, TMDLs, Section 6-1. The amendment\nL60@P1: established TMDLs for bacterial indicators in the Middle Santa Ana River, as well\nL61@P1: as wasteload allocations (WLAs) for point source discharges and load allocations\nL62@P1: (LAs) for non-point source discharges of bacterial indicators and a 10% margin of\nL63@P1: safety (MOS). The 2005 TMDLs also included an implementation plan,\nL64@P1: attainment schedule, and monitoring and reporting requirements.\nL65@P1: 8. During the process to adopt the 2005 TMDLs it was recognized that protection of\nL66@P1: recreational uses during dry weather was a higher priority than protection of\nL67@P1: recreational uses during wet weather – simply because recreational activity was\nL68@P1: more likely to occur during dry weather conditions. This prioritized approach to\nL69@P1: reduce or eliminate bacteria in dry weather flows has resulted in stakeholder\nL70@P1: resources being used for the construction of infrastructure to reduce bacteria to\nL71@P1: safe levels when recreational activity is most likely to occur, regardless of\nL72@P1: season.\nL73@P1: 9. The 2005 TMDLs established two schedules to comply with the TMDLs, WLAs\nL74@P1: and LAs for bacterial indicators in Middle Santa Ana River Watershed\nL75@P1: Waterbodies:\nL76@P1-2: · Dry Summer Conditions: April 1 through October 31, as soon as possible, but 3\nL77@P2: Resolution No. R8-2026-0014\nL78@P2: Page 3 of 7\nL79@P2: no later than December 31, 2015.\nL80@P2: · Wet Winter Conditions: November 1 through March 31, as soon as possible,\nL81@P2: but no later than December 31, 2025.\nL82@P2: 10.The Santa Ana Watershed Project Authority (SAWPA) is a joint power authority\nL83@P2: of five member agencies that supports water resources planning: Eastern\nL84@P2: Municipal Water District, Inland Empire Utilities Agency, Orange County Water\nL85@P2: District, San Bernardino Valley Municipal Water District, and Western Municipal\nL86@P2: Water District. In 2006, the MSAR TMDL Task Force (TMDL Task Force) was\nL87@P2: established, as a multi-agency, collaborative effort administered by SAWPA, to\nL88@P2: coordinate water quality improvement activities designed to support compliance\nL89@P2: with the MSAR TMDLs. The TMDL Task Force includes San Bernardino County\nL90@P2: Flood Control District (representing County of San Bernardino and Cities of\nL91@P2: Ontario, Chino, Chino Hills, Montclair, Rancho Cucamonga, Upland, Rialto, and\nL92@P2: Fontana); County of Riverside; Cities of Claremont, Corona, Eastvale, Jurupa\nL93@P2: Valley, Norco, Pomona and Riverside; and Agricultural Operators (represented\nL94@P2: by Chino Basin Watermaster) and University of California, Riverside. Since the\nL95@P2: adoption and approval of the 2005 TMDLs, stakeholders, working collectively\nL96@P2: through the Middle Santa Ana River TMDL Task Force, as part of a Municipal\nL97@P2: Separate Stormwater Sewer System (MS4) program, or as an individual\nL98@P2: permittee, have implemented numerous programs and projects to comply with\nL99@P2: the requirements set forth in the 2005 TMDLs’ Implementation Plan (Phase 1).\nL100@P2: 11.As of 2023, all the tasks originally set forth in the Phase 1 Implementation Plan\nL101@P2: have been implemented. Although impaired receiving waters are not attaining\nL102@P2: water quality objectives during dry weather flow conditions, the entities\nL103@P2: responsible for compliance with the WLAs and LAs remain committed to the\nL104@P2: continued implementation of their respective best management practices (BMP)\nL105@P2: based compliance plans, existing or as revised, to further reduce or eliminate\nL106@P2: sources of bacterial indicators during dry weather flow conditions.\nL107@P2: 12.To date, Middle Santa Ana River TMDL implementation activities have focused\nL108@P2: on efforts to comply with Dry Summer Conditions TMDLs when REC-1 activity is\nL109@P2: most likely to occur in the watershed. In addition, findings from studies conducted\nL110@P2: since 2007 have demonstrated the challenges of achieving compliance under dry\nL111@P2: weather conditions. Given the increased volume of water to be managed during\nL112@P2: wet weather, the challenge of complying with Wet Winter Conditions is expected\nL113@P2: to be orders of magnitude greater. Due to this prioritization and challenges,\nL114@P2: additional time is needed to meet the Wet Winter Conditions TMDLs, WLAs, and\nL115@P2: LAs.\nL116@P2: 13.The proposed revision will be limited to extending the compliance date\nL117@P2: associated with the Wet Winter Conditions TMDLs, WLAs and LAs from\n"
